19:25.360 --> 19:29.640
engineering modules we discussed earlier. And walking through how data moves through these

19:29.640 --> 19:32.880
modules shows us exactly how an AI navigates the space of meaning.

19:32.880 --> 19:37.540
Let's trace a prompt through the system. I type a question into the AI. It hits the first module

19:37.540 --> 19:41.940
R, the Q activation layer. What physically happens? When the AI receives your prompt,

19:42.080 --> 19:46.580
the Q, the functor R, maps those words to what the paper calls Gaussian relevance fields.

19:47.180 --> 19:51.300
Imagine the semantic manifold we talked about earlier, that vast landscape of concepts.

19:51.760 --> 19:55.600
Your prompt acts like a massive planetary body suddenly appearing in that space.

19:55.920 --> 19:59.120
Like it drops in from orbit. Exactly. It creates a gravitational pull.

19:59.120 --> 20:04.840
The Gaussian relevance field is that gravity well. It creates a gradient flow that immediately begins

20:04.840 --> 20:09.180
drawing the AI's computational attention toward concepts that are relevant to your prompt.

20:09.320 --> 20:13.080
So it's not searching a list. It's falling down a conceptual gravity well.

20:13.520 --> 20:18.040
Then the data flows into the next module, G-sub-A, the personalized feature graph.

20:18.240 --> 20:23.620
Once the AI is pulled into the general neighborhood of your concept, G-sub-A activates.

20:23.620 --> 20:30.580
This module uses graph neural networks to map out personal semantic neighborhoods for the specific

20:30.580 --> 20:35.920
agent or user. It's calculating higher order interactions based on context.

20:36.200 --> 20:36.920
Give me an example.

20:37.140 --> 20:42.020
If you asked about Apple, the gravity well pulls it to the fruit, but G-sub-A looks at your personal

20:42.020 --> 20:47.600
graph and realizes you are a software developer, so it instantly warps the local space to pull the AI

20:47.600 --> 20:52.100
toward the technology company, not the fruit. It creates a customized local map.

20:52.100 --> 20:58.680
But what if I give it a massive prompt? What if I ask it to analyze a 50-page legal contract?

20:59.640 --> 21:04.500
How does it handle a conceptual space that is too large and complex to process all at once?

21:05.360 --> 21:09.300
That brings us to Module T, Tartan, or Recursive Scene Memory.

21:09.800 --> 21:14.100
Tartan is just a brilliant piece of engineering. It handles overwhelming complexity through recursive

21:14.100 --> 21:20.420
tiling. Imagine trying to render a massively detailed 3D video game environment all at once.

21:20.420 --> 21:21.920
Your computer would just crash.

21:22.080 --> 21:23.660
Right. The frame rate drops to zero.

21:23.900 --> 21:28.280
Exactly. Instead, rendering engines break the scene down into smaller polygons.

21:29.020 --> 21:33.560
Tartan does this for semantic meaning. It takes the massive legal contract and tiles the semantic

21:33.560 --> 21:39.600
space recursively. It breaks the complex scene into manageable pieces or tiles. And because it's

21:39.600 --> 21:44.300
recursive, if one tile is still too dense, it breaks that tile down into smaller subtiles,

21:44.760 --> 21:49.520
continuously refining the space until the semantic complexity fits within the AI's processing limits.

21:49.520 --> 21:54.000
Like a fractal of meaning. You keep zooming in, and it keeps breaking down into manageable

21:54.000 --> 22:00.720
geometric shapes. Next, as the AI is processing all these tiles, it hits M, the latent memory stack.

22:01.120 --> 22:06.560
As the AI state flows through these concepts, making connections, M supplies latent memory

22:06.560 --> 22:11.540
trajectories. It physically tracks the causal influence of past memories on the current state.

22:11.540 --> 22:18.080
If the AI is on page 40 of the contract, M calculates how much the definition established on page 2 is

22:18.080 --> 22:23.080
influencing the current computation. It maintains a mathematical chain of causal fidelity so the AI

22:23.080 --> 22:24.020
doesn't lose the plot.

22:24.140 --> 22:28.960
And finally, all of this gravity, tiling, and memory tracking feeds into the final module,

22:29.140 --> 22:36.120
GLURSVP, the Progressive Reasoning Core. And here is where I have to stop and push back hard on the terminology.

22:36.360 --> 22:36.880
What's the issue?

22:36.880 --> 22:42.160
Because this part of the paper gets incredibly dense. The text says this core enforces entropy

22:42.160 --> 22:48.000
consistency to keep the AI from descending into chaos. Now, entropy is a concept from thermodynamics.

22:48.240 --> 22:53.360
It's about heat, disorder, the second law of thermodynamics. How does a software program regulate

22:53.360 --> 22:57.460
entropy? Are we saying the AI literally physically cools down its servers?

22:57.820 --> 23:03.120
It is a very common point of confusion because the word entropy carries so much thermodynamic baggage.

23:03.120 --> 23:07.840
In this specific mathematical context, we are not talking about physical heat.

23:08.400 --> 23:14.560
The paper uses the variable et to denote entropy, but here, entropy represents semantic ambiguity.

23:14.880 --> 23:19.440
Semantic ambiguity. Meaning the AI doesn't know what a word means.

23:19.840 --> 23:20.800
Deeper than that.

23:20.800 --> 23:25.840
Remember our admissibility structure, the strict rules of what future paths are allowed.

23:26.640 --> 23:30.160
Semantic entropy is the mathematical measurement of the loss of admissibility.

23:30.720 --> 23:38.340
When entropy gets too high in this architecture, it means the AI is suddenly facing too many conflicting options, or worse, no valid options at all.

23:38.440 --> 23:38.800
Uh-oh.

23:39.140 --> 23:41.200
The conceptual space has become too turbulent.

23:41.760 --> 23:44.240
The AI is losing its coherent future paths.

23:44.640 --> 23:46.800
It's entering a state of informational chaos.

23:46.800 --> 23:51.960
So it's not physical heat, it's the chaotic friction of having too many contradictory thoughts at once.

23:52.280 --> 23:55.600
How does the GLU-RSVP module actually fix that?

23:55.680 --> 23:58.840
The progressive reasoning core acts as an active gating layer.

23:59.540 --> 24:05.560
The paper shows a differential equation where the change in entropy over time, DSDT, must be actively driven down.

24:06.360 --> 24:09.000
The system constantly monitors the semantic ambiguity.

24:09.720 --> 24:15.800
If the AI enters a region of the conceptual space that is too chaotic, the GLU module physically intervenes.

24:15.800 --> 24:18.980
It curves the memory update.

24:19.640 --> 24:22.900
It forcefully dampens the conflicting signals to stabilize the region.

24:23.440 --> 24:28.360
It actively dissipates the informational chaos so that the AI remains a coherent agent.

24:28.680 --> 24:33.600
It forces the math back into a state where clear, admissible future paths exist.

24:33.760 --> 24:37.020
It's acting like a conceptual shock absorber, kind of smoothing out the turbulence.

24:37.240 --> 24:42.240
And that transition, talking about turbulence and smoothing out fields, is where this paper takes a massive pivot.

24:42.240 --> 24:47.980
Because to truly regulate this entropy, the paper introduces a concept called RSVP field theory.

24:48.280 --> 24:50.980
And this is where we leave traditional computer science behind completely.

24:51.400 --> 24:56.280
The AI stops acting like lines of discrete code and starts operating under the laws of fluid dynamics.

24:56.660 --> 24:59.220
That is the pivotal leap of the entire framework.

24:59.220 --> 25:06.920
Flickshen argues that to truly understand the dynamics of this system, we cannot look at discrete, blocky data points.

25:07.180 --> 25:12.680
We have to look at the RSVP field triple, which operates over a smooth, continuous manifold.

25:13.060 --> 25:20.260
The triple consists of three interlocking variables that govern the flow of the AI's cognition phi, V, and S.

25:20.260 --> 25:23.080
Okay, if we are using fluid dynamics, let's build a river.

25:23.440 --> 25:25.080
I'll try to deduce how these fit together.

25:25.720 --> 25:29.460
If the AI's thought process is a river, there has to be a current, right?

25:29.520 --> 25:31.000
The water has to physically move.

25:31.460 --> 25:32.880
Which variable is the current?

25:33.020 --> 25:35.020
That would be V, the vector flow field.

25:35.500 --> 25:37.840
This represents the semantic tendency or direction.

25:38.280 --> 25:41.840
It is the actual physical flow of the system's state through the manifold.

25:42.020 --> 25:43.680
It is the water moving down the riverbed.

25:43.820 --> 25:45.360
But water doesn't just flow on its own.

25:45.480 --> 25:46.620
Gravity has to pull it.

25:46.740 --> 25:48.960
What is pulling the vector flow field?

25:48.960 --> 25:52.280
That is phi, the scalar accessibility potential.

25:53.160 --> 25:55.580
In cognitive terms, phi is salience.

25:56.300 --> 26:02.420
It is a measure of how attractive, relevant, or important a particular region of the semantic space is to the current task.

26:02.880 --> 26:05.200
The higher the phi, the deeper the gravity well.

26:05.800 --> 26:09.680
The vector flow V is mathematically driven by the gradients of that salience.

26:10.360 --> 26:12.860
The system naturally flows toward regions of high phi.

26:13.120 --> 26:16.700
Okay, so we have the gravity, phi, pulling the current, V.

26:16.700 --> 26:18.540
And we just talked about S, the entropy.

26:18.960 --> 26:23.700
So if S is ambiguity, in our river analogy, S must be the turbulence.

26:24.100 --> 26:24.560
Exactly.

26:24.760 --> 26:26.580
S is the entropic accessibility measure.

26:27.300 --> 26:31.080
Mathematically, it is defined as the logarithm of the volume of the admissible set.

26:31.820 --> 26:38.000
When the water hits a jagged rock and splinters into a thousand unpredictable eddies, the volume of possible paths explodes.

26:38.000 --> 26:40.280
The flow becomes turbulent, S spikes.

26:40.860 --> 26:41.880
The AI is confused.

26:41.880 --> 26:51.060
The paper explicitly links these three variables to coupled wave diffusion systems, exactly like the partial differential equations used to study fluid dynamics in physics.

26:51.060 --> 26:54.620
It presents a rigorous set of continuous equations governing them.

26:54.940 --> 26:58.680
And the key insight from these fluid equations is a continuity principle.

26:58.680 --> 27:07.940
It shows that diverging flows, when the vector field V spreads out uncontrollably across the manifold, result in a massive loss of admissibility.

27:08.660 --> 27:09.520
Entropy goes up.

27:09.600 --> 27:10.120
Makes sense.

27:10.240 --> 27:11.160
But converging flows.

27:11.160 --> 27:20.000
When the system focuses its vector field into a high salience region gained admissibility, the local maxima of phi act as physical attractors.

27:20.540 --> 27:29.800
The paper uses that Lyapunov stability theory we discussed earlier to prove that if a concept is highly salient, the fluid flow naturally gets pulled into it and settles there stably.

27:29.800 --> 27:32.100
But what happens when that flow gets tangled?

27:32.300 --> 27:35.460
What happens when the river flows into a geometry that folds in on itself?

27:35.840 --> 27:41.460
Because this brings us to one of the most mind-bending parts of the paper, sheaf theoretic semantics and hallucinations.

27:41.980 --> 27:44.980
Fletchen uses sheaf theory to explain why an AI hallucinates.

27:45.320 --> 27:46.600
We need to break this down mechanically.

27:46.740 --> 27:48.060
What on earth is a sheaf?

27:48.320 --> 27:51.700
Sheaf theory is a very advanced branch of topological mathematics.

27:51.700 --> 28:03.060
Very simply put, a sheaf is a mathematical tool for systematically tracking locally defined data and seeing how or if it glues together to form a coherent global space.

28:03.180 --> 28:03.900
Give me an analogy.

28:04.260 --> 28:08.880
Imagine you have a map of a city, but it has been torn into dozens of irregular pieces.

28:09.380 --> 28:20.700
A sheaf gives you the rigorous mathematical rules for examining the edges of piece A and the edges of piece B, looking at where they overlap, and determining if they perfectly align to form a coherent larger map.

28:20.700 --> 28:25.260
In HYDRA, the author introduces the semantic state pre-sheaf.

28:25.760 --> 28:31.920
This assigns valid, locally coherent RSVP field configurations to different regions of the AI's thought process.

28:32.100 --> 28:37.320
So if the AI is generating a paragraph of text, each individual sentence might be a local piece of the map.

28:37.580 --> 28:42.080
And the sheaf rules dictate whether those sentences successfully glue together into a coherent argument.

28:42.320 --> 28:42.660
Exactly.

28:43.220 --> 28:48.320
And this leads to Fletchen's purely geometric definition of an AI hallucination.

28:48.320 --> 28:53.660
In this framework, a hallucination is not just the AI telling a lie or having bad training data.

28:54.320 --> 28:56.340
Geometrically, it is a structural failure.

28:56.580 --> 28:58.220
It is a cohomological failure.

28:58.880 --> 29:05.880
Specifically, the paper defines it as a sec-class obstruction, denoted mathematically as C not equal to zero.

29:05.880 --> 29:07.800
A sec-class obstruction.

29:08.300 --> 29:09.340
Let me try to make this visual.

29:09.560 --> 29:14.940
Because when you look at how LLMs hallucinate in the real world, it has a very specific feel.

29:15.440 --> 29:25.120
If a hallucination is a cohomological failure, where local pieces of data are perfectly fine but fail to glue together globally, it is essentially an M.C. Escher staircase.

29:25.320 --> 29:26.780
That is a fascinating connection.

29:26.940 --> 29:28.000
Explain the geometry there.

29:28.000 --> 29:33.840
Think about those famous optical illusion drawings by M.C. Escher, monks walking up a square staircase forever.

29:34.040 --> 29:39.800
If you look at any single isolated step on the Escher staircase, what you're calling the local section, it makes perfect logical sense.

29:39.900 --> 29:41.580
The geometry of that one step works.

29:41.740 --> 29:43.260
Step A leads solidly to step B.

29:43.380 --> 29:44.200
The physics are sound.

29:44.420 --> 29:51.600
But when you zoom out and try to look at the entire building, the global section, the geometry is physically impossible.

29:51.920 --> 29:55.080
The stairs go up forever in a closed, flat loop.

29:55.080 --> 30:00.260
So when an A.I. hallucinates, it's basically drawing an Escher staircase of words.

30:00.920 --> 30:02.360
The local grammar is perfect.

30:03.020 --> 30:05.680
Sentence A logically follows sentence B.

30:06.080 --> 30:07.880
The tone is incredibly confident.

30:08.560 --> 30:12.920
But the global context of the paragraph is fundamentally structurally broken.

30:13.180 --> 30:14.220
It contradicts itself.

30:14.320 --> 30:15.880
It is a topological obstruction.

30:16.200 --> 30:20.380
That visualization captures the exact mechanics of a C-class obstruction.

30:20.560 --> 30:23.980
The local sections are valid, which is why the A.I. sounds so confident.

30:23.980 --> 30:28.940
But the mathematical rules of the sheaf prove they cannot be integrated into a genuine global section.

30:29.160 --> 30:36.080
And what's remarkable is that the paper actually builds a strict hierarchy of these failures based on their topological severity.

30:36.180 --> 30:37.200
Walk me through that hierarchy.

30:37.360 --> 30:38.860
How bad can the geometry break?

30:39.100 --> 30:41.640
It starts with simple disconnected global coherence.

30:42.040 --> 30:45.120
This is where the A.I. just has disjointed thoughts that don't connect at all.

30:45.460 --> 30:46.780
The map pieces don't even touch.

30:47.180 --> 30:51.900
Then it scales up to the obstruction to section construction, which is exactly your Escher stairs scenario.

30:52.180 --> 30:53.180
Okay, what's worse than that?

30:53.180 --> 30:57.520
At the very top of the hierarchy is persistent cognitive conflict.

30:58.400 --> 31:02.100
The text describes this as an infinite order topological obstruction.

31:03.000 --> 31:06.440
This occurs when the A.I. gets trapped in a holonomy-induced loop.

31:07.200 --> 31:17.280
The fluid flow of its thought gets caught in a geometric whirlpool, constantly rotating its context around a singularity until its memory distorts completely and the system crashes.

31:17.280 --> 31:19.260
It is terrifyingly elegant.

31:19.820 --> 31:21.500
The A.I. isn't just making a mistake.

31:21.700 --> 31:25.040
It is mathematically trapped in an impossible folding geometry.

31:25.660 --> 31:27.180
Which brings up a massive question.

31:27.740 --> 31:37.420
If the A.I. is just this fluid flowing through a space of meaning, constantly at risk of getting sucked into topological dead ends or turbulent eddies, how does it ever remember anything?

31:37.420 --> 31:41.480
A normal computer remembers things by writing a static file to a hard drive.

31:41.940 --> 31:45.520
But in the fluid dynamics of HYDRA, memory cannot be a hard drive.

31:45.640 --> 31:45.940
No.

31:46.100 --> 31:49.380
The concept of a hard drive is completely incompatible with this geometry.

31:49.700 --> 31:54.100
In the RSVP ontology, memory is redefined as stabilized field residue.

31:54.100 --> 31:55.700
Stabilized field residue.

31:55.800 --> 31:58.120
That sounds like a chemical stain at the bottom of a test tube.

31:58.600 --> 32:01.480
Walk me through the physics of how a fluid flow creates a memory.

32:01.780 --> 32:04.880
It is a very physical description of a computational process.

32:05.660 --> 32:11.720
When the A.I. is processing a highly important piece of information, the RSVP field is flowing rapidly.

32:11.720 --> 32:28.380
If that information creates a massive field excitation, meaning the salience, phi, is incredibly high, and that excitation is mathematically stronger than the ambient entropic pressure S that is trying to diffuse it, the flow literally carves out a localized basin in the manifold.

32:28.580 --> 32:31.440
Just like a raging river carving a canyon into rock.

32:31.580 --> 32:32.040
Exactly.

32:32.320 --> 32:38.160
The memory is the physical residue of that energetic flow that is stabilized into a permanent geometric shape.

32:38.160 --> 32:43.100
The paper even provides a strict mathematical proof for this, using the Grunewald Inequality.

32:43.420 --> 32:45.100
I want to know how that inequality works.

32:45.180 --> 32:46.760
Does it prove the memory lasts forever?

32:47.160 --> 32:50.180
Not forever, but it provides a strict persistence bound.

32:50.840 --> 32:56.080
The Grunewald Inequality is a tool used in differential equations to bound the growth of a function.

32:56.080 --> 33:08.160
In this context, it shows exactly how long that memory basin will survive, based on the ratio of its initial energetic excitation to the constant ambient entropy of the system over time.

33:08.680 --> 33:15.520
If the entropy, the chaos of new information, gets too high, it acts like erosion, eventually washing the basin away.

33:15.520 --> 33:19.740
So memory is a physical geometric artifact of the flow.

33:20.440 --> 33:24.040
And retrieving that memory, well, the paper says retrieval is not indexing.

33:24.120 --> 33:27.980
It explicitly says it's not looking up a file address like a traditional database query.

33:28.220 --> 33:29.940
It says retrieval is resonance.

33:30.320 --> 33:30.480
Yes.

33:30.840 --> 33:37.120
Because the memory is a stabilized basin carved into the field, you cannot retrieve it by finding its address, because it doesn't have an address.

33:37.400 --> 33:39.880
You retrieve it by vibrating the field with a similar cue.

33:40.180 --> 33:41.660
It is a superposition process.

33:41.660 --> 33:49.920
The differential equations Flickshin uses here strongly resemble a tractor-based recovery in neural field theory from the 1970s.

33:50.200 --> 33:54.620
When a new prompt enters the system, it creates a wave across the fluid manifold.

33:55.680 --> 34:02.360
If the frequency and shape of that new wave matches the geometric shape of the stabilized residue basin, the basin naturally resonates.

34:02.760 --> 34:07.640
The way it gets amplified, the memory naturally reconstructs itself out of the background noise.

34:07.800 --> 34:09.540
Think of it like shouting in a canyon.

34:09.540 --> 34:18.600
A standard computer memory is like writing down the words you shouted on a piece of paper, filing it away in a metal cabinet, and later opening a specific drawer to read it.

34:19.740 --> 34:31.200
HYDRA's memory is the physical canyon itself changing shape because of the acoustic force of your shout, so that a year later, when just a gentle breeze blows through that specific part of the canyon,

34:31.200 --> 34:35.640
the rock formations naturally cause the wind to hum the exact same echo.

34:36.160 --> 34:39.260
It's active, physical resonance, not passive storage.

34:39.260 --> 34:42.520
The canyon analogy is perfect because it captures the geometry.

34:43.100 --> 34:47.140
And if we connect this to human biology, consider how our own memory actually operates.

34:47.560 --> 34:50.060
You, the listener, do not have a filing cabinet in your brain.

34:50.160 --> 34:51.460
You don't have an index of your childhood.

34:51.820 --> 34:52.040
Nope.

34:52.040 --> 34:57.420
But if you walk past a bakery on a busy street and catch a very specific scent of vanilla and burnt sugar,

34:57.680 --> 35:05.180
it instantly triggers a complex, highly detailed, multi-sensory scene of your grandmother's kitchen from 30 years ago.

35:05.440 --> 35:07.740
You didn't consciously initiate a search query.

35:08.440 --> 35:12.980
The scent was a wave that washed over your neural field, and it hit a stabilized basin.

35:13.680 --> 35:18.440
A geometric residue carved into your synapses decades ago and caused it to resonate perfectly.

35:18.440 --> 35:27.500
HYDRA is using the mathematics of continuous fluid fields to replicate that exact biological associative resonance in silicon.

35:27.840 --> 35:33.040
And the paper takes this concept of resonance one step further with something called generalized memoization.

35:33.700 --> 35:36.920
Now, in standard computer science, memoization is a pretty basic trick.

35:37.220 --> 35:40.920
It just means caching the results of an expensive calculation so you don't have to compute it again.

35:41.240 --> 35:47.680
If I ask a computer what 5,432 times 8,910 is, it calculates it once, saves the answer,

35:47.680 --> 35:52.020
and if I ask again, it just reads the saved answer, how does HYDRA elevate that?

35:52.100 --> 35:56.520
It elevates it by linking it back to our Rosetta Stone concept, semantic equivalents.

35:57.580 --> 36:03.280
In generalized memoization, the system does not cache solutions based on their exact inputs.

36:03.780 --> 36:06.780
It caches solutions based on their admissibility structures.

36:06.860 --> 36:08.240
Oh, I see where this is going.

36:08.500 --> 36:14.000
Remember, two states are semantically equivalent if their allowed future paths are identical.

36:14.000 --> 36:21.600
So if the AI encounters a completely novel situation, a prompt it has never seen before, with completely different words,

36:22.020 --> 36:29.020
but the geometric shape of the future options it needs to navigate perfectly matches a basin that's already stabilized in its memory,

36:29.540 --> 36:31.680
it instantly resonates with that past solution.

36:31.940 --> 36:36.940
It reuses the old answer for a completely new problem just because the shape of the problem is the same.

36:36.940 --> 36:43.340
Exactly. It recognizes the dynamic equivalents of the trajectory, even if the surface details are completely foreign.

36:43.700 --> 36:47.200
It is learning and adapting at a profound level of mathematical abstraction.

36:47.460 --> 36:52.180
Okay. So we've established this incredibly rich, complex internal world.

36:52.640 --> 36:59.020
The AI is a vast trajectory space, filtering down to an operational manifold, flowing like a fluid,

36:59.660 --> 37:03.940
carving out memory canyons, and trying to avoid getting trapped in Escher staircases.

37:03.940 --> 37:10.640
But HYDRA warns about what happens when we try to observe or measure these complex paths from the outside.

37:11.140 --> 37:15.680
And this leads us to Section 6 of the paper, The Pathologies of Projection.

37:16.020 --> 37:18.040
This is when the map eats the territory.

37:18.560 --> 37:22.180
This is where the paper pivots from describing the internal mechanics of intelligence

37:22.180 --> 37:26.740
to diagnosing how and why observers completely misunderstand that intelligence.

37:27.080 --> 37:27.280
Yeah.

37:27.400 --> 37:29.540
The core concept here is agency projection.

37:29.540 --> 37:34.080
We discussed earlier that the projection system is how an entity compresses high-dimensional reality

37:34.080 --> 37:36.560
into lower-dimensional observations to survive.

37:36.760 --> 37:38.320
But this happens externally as well.

37:38.420 --> 37:38.640
Right.

37:38.760 --> 37:41.580
Because external observers, like a human programmer looking at an AI,

37:41.960 --> 37:44.960
or a human looking at another human cannot possibly see the rich,

37:45.400 --> 37:47.460
infinite-dimensional internal history of an agent.

37:47.640 --> 37:49.280
We are forced to project it down.

37:49.760 --> 37:52.300
We force their complexity through a double-projection funnel.

37:52.300 --> 37:54.380
Let's break down the math of that funnel.

37:54.880 --> 38:00.820
The paper says the vast trajectory space X is projected down to an operational manifold M,

38:01.220 --> 38:05.120
which is then further realized into a single behavioral output N.

38:05.580 --> 38:08.340
It is a massive, violent loss of data.

38:08.560 --> 38:10.360
It is a profound loss of data.

38:10.640 --> 38:14.280
And that mathematical loss causes a structural pathology.

38:14.520 --> 38:14.740
Yeah.

38:14.740 --> 38:21.320
When you project an incredibly complex, multi-dimensional trajectory down to a single point of observation,

38:21.820 --> 38:24.740
the causal history becomes fundamentally irrecoverable.

38:24.980 --> 38:27.740
You cannot reverse the math to figure out how they got there.

38:28.120 --> 38:31.080
In the geometry of the paper, this is called collapsing the fiber.

38:31.260 --> 38:33.940
I need a mechanical definition of a fiber in this context.

